# Example reverse proxy in front of the two-bot staging deploy broker (TOG-6911).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Deploy jobs run on ubuntu-latest (the repo is public, #304;
# stale docs naming private self-hosted runners are not permission to switch
# back). A hosted runner cannot dial the broker's loopback bind (127.0.0.1:8091
# in server.mjs — a deploy authority never listens on a public interface), so
# the host terminates public TLS and forwards at root to loopback. The proxy is
# UNTRUSTED transport: every authority check (Bearer token, pinned staging app
# UUID, repo/SHA validation, redaction) lives in the broker itself, so a proxy
# misconfiguration can deny service but cannot deploy production or mint
# authority. The broker token does travel through the proxy, so the proxy must
# terminate TLS (never forward over plaintext) and must not log headers/bodies.
#
# OPERATOR-ONLY. Agents never install this: it binds public ports and carries
# the public origin. The operator picks the real public hostname (a name the box
# already serves TLS for, or a new one), provisions
# STAGING_BROKER_URL=https://<that-hostname> as the Actions secret through
# TOG-8272, installs the proxy, and verifies with the checks below. Source
# changes stay with agents; the operator deploys the reviewed artifact.
#
# PROXY CONTRACT (any mechanism — Caddy, a Traefik route, a tunnel — must
# satisfy every item; the Caddy block at the bottom is one known-good shape):
#   1. Public origin is https:// only. Plaintext http:// from outside is
#      refused or redirected — never a 200 (the broker token never travels
#      over plaintext; the clients refuse it too, see resolveBrokerUrl).
#   2. Forwards at ROOT. GET /healthz on the public origin must return the
#      broker's {"ok":true,"service":"two-staging-broker"}. A path prefix
#      (e.g. /broker/...) breaks every broker route — clients send absolute
#      paths and the broker has no prefix stripping, on purpose.
#   3. Forwards the Authorization header byte-identical (the broker's
#      constant-time Bearer check fails closed on any rewrite).
#   4. No access/error logging of headers or bodies (the Bearer token is in
#      every request). Status line + status code only.
#   5. Forwards ONLY to 127.0.0.1:8091. No other backend, no fallback.
#   6. Staging only: this proxy never fronts a production deploy path.
#      Production stays HOLD until TOG-6903.
#
# VERIFY (operator, from anywhere with curl — this is the hosted-runner view):
#   curl -s https://<broker-host>/healthz
#     # {"ok":true,"service":"two-staging-broker"}
#   curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://<broker-host>/v1/staging/app
#     # 401 (auth reaches the broker; the body is the static code only)
#   curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://<broker-host>/healthz
#     # 301/308 to https://, or connection refused — never a 200 over plaintext
#
# ROLLBACK: remove the site block and reload the proxy. Staging deploys then go
# red naming the unreachable broker (TOG-913) — nothing silently deploys, and
# production was never reachable through this proxy.
#
# --- Caddy example (the contract above is normative; this block is illustrative)
#
# Replace broker.example.invalid with the real public hostname, drop this block
# into the Caddyfile, and `caddy reload` (or `systemctl reload caddy`).
# Automatic TLS on first request. Caddy's default access log carries method,
# URI, status and size — no headers, no bodies; keep it that way (never enable
# header/body debug logging on this site).
#
# broker.example.invalid {
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8091
# }
